From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  5 20:33:17 2011
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 05 Dec 2011 20:33:17 +0000
Subject: function template defaults
Message-ID: <2NMIGNHTRi4.pR-Zn5L4hN4J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/2NMIGNHTRi4/m/pR-Zn5L4hN4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Daniel and I came up with a way to merge the
policied and unpolicied function calls using
template defaults.  Daniel then went and pushed
this through the library.  It all worked fine
on our Macs.  Then he tried other compilers and
platforms, which couldn't handle the function
template defaults.

The current standard allows defaults for class
template parameters, but not for function template
parameters.

Eventually, the compilers should catch up to the
new C++11 standard, but I think it'd be nice to run
with the current crop until then.

So I'm suggesting that we switch our functions back
to Boost style for now at least, such as

template <typename T1, typename T2>
inline typename calc<T2,T3>::type
foo(const T1& x1, const T2& x2) {
     return foo(x1,x2,policy<>::type);
}

template <typename T1, typename T2, typename P>
inline typename calc<T1,T2>::type
foo(const T1& x1, const T2& x2, const P& /* ignore */) {
     ... use P() if necessary ...
}

The inline combined with the resolution of the
default policy at compile time and the second function
not paying attention to the actual param means there's
at least no run-time overhead.

The downside is that there's now double the doc
to write, though I think the resulting unpolicied function,
which is what you almost always want to use, will be
clearer to most users.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  5 20:55:32 2011
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 05 Dec 2011 20:55:32 +0000
Subject: expressions on LHS of sampling statements
Message-ID: <CINuKmHyYFg.h46fYX_vmzIJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/CINuKmHyYFg/m/h46fYX_vmzIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I just committed changes to Stan to allow
general expressions on the LHS of sampling
statements.  This should save some derived
data and parameter definitions and (I hope)
make the models easier to read.

The general form for sampling statements is now:

    expr ~ distribution(expr[1],...,expr[N]);

This means we can do things like write:

    1 ~ bernoulli(theta);

instead of what we'd have to do in BUGS:

    x <- 1
    x ~ bernoulli(theta);

Similarly, if we want the binomial prior from
BDA (which I know Andrew doesn't like any more), we
could write:

   a / (a + b) ~ uniform(0,1);
   a + b ~ pareto(1.5,0.5);
   for (n in 1:N)
       theta[n] ~ beta(a,b);

instead of:

   phi ~ uniform(0,1);
   kappa ~ pareto(1.5,0.5);
   a <- kappa * phi;
   b <- kappa * (1 - phi);
   for (n in 1:N)
       theta[n] ~ beta(a,b);

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  5 21:09:53 2011
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Mon, 05 Dec 2011 21:09:53 +0000
Subject: censoring w/o cumulative densities
Message-ID: <Qe_7Wr1cKHs.GNqaHKT3pDUJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/Qe_7Wr1cKHs/m/GNqaHKT3pDUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We can use Stan's constrained variables if we sample latent
parameters corresponding to censored measurements:

data {
     double C;

     int(0,) N_c;
     double x_c[N_c];

     int(0,) N_uc;
     double x_uc[N_uc];
     double(,C) y_uc[N_uc];
...
}
parameters {
     double(C,) y_c[N_c];
...
}
model {
     for (n in 1:N_c)
         y_c[n] ~ normal(alpha * x_c[n] + beta, sigma);

     for (n in 1:N_uc)
         y_uc[n] ~ normal(alpha * x_uc[n] + beta, sigma);
     ...
}

The uncensored data is already in the proper range (the
ranges on the data are just for error checking the data
input).

The declaration double(C,) for the censored data vector y_c
forces each y_c[n] to be sampled from the range (C,inf).

The version using the cumulative density removes the
parameter y_c and replaces its sampling loop with:

     for (n in 1:N_c)
         1 ~ bernoulli(c_normal_lb(alpha * x_c[n] + beta, sigma, C));

This formulation effectively integrates out the parameter
vector y_c.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Mon Dec  5 23:31:15 2011
