From u1004dd6f66bb10c3 at groups.google.com  Wed Dec 28 16:56:42 2011
From: u1004dd6f66bb10c3 at groups.google.com (Matt Hoffman)
Date: Wed, 28 Dec 2011 16:56:42 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] evaluating 0 * log(0) in log probabilities
Message-ID: <oq0nPFHdvP4.Q2rMawmEKccJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <oq0nPFHdvP4.dBbn_VdL4A0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <oq0nPFHdvP4.dBbn_VdL4A0J@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/oq0nPFHdvP4/m/Q2rMawmEKccJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0*log(0) comes up all the time (e.g. when assigning 0 probability to
an unobserved event), and we almost always want it to be 0, since for
most distributions we care about it makes no practical difference if
θ=0 or θ=1e-100, and the limit when approaching 0*log(0)=0.

I don't know of any (non-pathological) subtleties to do with this that
could bite us; if any exist, my opinion is that they're unlikely to
cause us more trouble than letting 0*log(0)=NaN.

Matt

